    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i was worried bone meal might throw off the ph too much since it's got more phosphorus. can you explain the actual chemical reaction or how it works to release the nitrogen compared to blood meal? also, how long does it usually take to see a difference in the leaves after application?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Blood meal and bone meal each contain different forms of nitrogen: blood meal is nearly 12% nitrogen by weight with a nitrogen to phosphorus (N:P) ratio of 3:1, while bone meal, derived from animal bones, contains 10-12% available nitrogen and has a nitrogen to phosphorus ratio closer to 1:2. The chemical reaction in the soil involving blood meal involves its metabolism into ammonium (NH4+) and nitrate (NO3-) forms that plants can readily absorb, with the nitrogen being directly available for uptake because it is in an easily accessible form.\n\n\nBone meal's decomposition is slower than blood meal's. It first dissolves slowly, releasing nutrients over time. As it breaks down, it becomes increasingly available to plants. The rate of nitrogen release from blood meal can be more immediate, potentially showing signs of improvement in newer, more recently matured leaves within a couple of weeks, as opposed to the slower and more gradual effects of bone meal.\n\n\nIt's essential to be cautious with the application rates to avoid phytotoxicity, which could manifest as leaf burn or discoloration, particularly when applying blood meal due to its high nitrogen concentration. A soil test before application will help you fine-tune the amount to match your soil",
      "source": "target",
